The JavaScript code you provided is attempting to execute a command using the `system` function, which is typically used to run shell commands on Unix-like operating systems (such as Linux or macOS). However, this code will not work in a web browser environment because the `system` function is not available there.

In a web browser, you would need to use a different approach to achieve similar functionality, such as making an AJAX request to a server that can handle the command and return the result.

Here's an example of how you might do this in a browser environment:

1. Create a server-side script (e.g., a Node.js server) that runs the `whoami` command.
2. Make an AJAX request from your JavaScript code to the server.

### Example Server-Side Script (Node.js)

```javascript
const http = require('http');
const os = require('os');

const hostname = '127.0.0.1';
const port = 3000;

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
    if (req.url === '/whoami') {
        const username = os.userInfo().username;
        res.statusCode = 200;
        res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/plain');
        res.end(username);
    } else {
        res.statusCode = 404;
        res.end('Not Found');
    }
});

server.listen(port, hostname, () => {
    console.log(`Server running at http://${hostname}:${port}/`);
});
```

### Example Client-Side JavaScript

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Who Am I?</title>
</head>
<body>
    <script>
        fetch('http://127.0.0.1:3000/whoami')
           .then(response => response.text())
           .then(data => {
                console.log('Username:', data);
                document.body.innerHTML = `<p>Your username is: ${data}</p>`;
            })
           .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This example sets up a simple HTTP server with a `/whoami` endpoint that returns the current user's username. The client-side JavaScript makes an AJAX request to this endpoint and logs the result to the console.

